# Code Quality Standards

## Linting and Formatting

### ESLint

Run linter before commits:

```bash
npm run lint
```

Fix auto-fixable issues:

```bash
npm run lint:code -- --fix
```

**Rules:**

- Follow `@typescript-eslint/recommended` ruleset
- Enable Prettier integration via `eslint-config-prettier`
- No warnings allowed in production code
- Fix lint errors before committing

### Prettier

Format all TypeScript files:

```bash
npm run format
```

Check formatting without changes:

```bash
npm run format:check
```

**Configuration:**

- 2-space indentation
- Single quotes for strings
- Trailing commas in multiline structures
- 100 character line length
- Semicolons required

## Pre-commit Checklist

Before committing code, ensure:

- [ ] `npm run compile` succeeds
- [ ] `npm run lint` passes with no errors
- [ ] `npm run format:check` passes
- [ ] `npm test` all tests pass
- [ ] No `console.log()` debugging statements
- [ ] No commented-out code blocks
- [ ] TODO comments for incomplete work
- [ ] Updated documentation if API changed

## Code Review Standards

When reviewing code, check for:

### Architecture

- Follows project structure patterns
- Proper separation of concerns
- Appropriate abstraction levels
- No circular dependencies

### TypeScript Quality

- Proper type annotations
- No `any` types without justification
- Interfaces for all public APIs
- Proper null/undefined handling

### Error Handling

- Try/catch for async operations
- User-friendly error messages
- Errors logged to output channel
- No swallowed exceptions

### Performance

- No unnecessary re-renders
- Efficient data structures
- Debounced rapid updates
- Lazy loading where appropriate

### Security

- Input validation
- Proper HTML escaping in webviews
- No hardcoded credentials
- Safe file operations

### Testability

- Functions are unit-testable
- Dependencies can be mocked
- No global state when avoidable
- Tests included for new features

## Documentation Requirements

### Code Comments

- JSDoc for all exported functions/classes
- Inline comments for complex logic
- Explain _why_, not _what_
- Link to relevant issues/PRs

### README Updates

Update [README.md](mdc:README.md) when:

- Adding new commands
- Changing extension behavior
- Adding configuration options
- Updating requirements

### CHANGELOG

Update [CHANGELOG.md](mdc:CHANGELOG.md) for:

- New features
- Bug fixes
- Breaking changes
- Deprecations

Follow [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/) format.

## Git Commit Standards

### Commit Message Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<body>

<footer>
```

**Types:**

- `feat`: New feature
- `fix`: Bug fix
- `docs`: Documentation changes
- `style`: Code style changes (formatting)
- `refactor`: Code refactoring
- `test`: Adding/updating tests
- `chore`: Build process or tooling changes

**Examples:**

```
feat(views): add thought tree visualization

Implement TreeDataProvider for displaying sequential thinking
process in VS Code sidebar. Includes collapsible nodes and
real-time updates.

Closes #12
```

```
fix(mcp): handle connection timeout gracefully

Add retry logic and user notification when MCP server
connection times out. Prevents extension crash.
```

### Branch Naming

- `feat/` - New features
- `fix/` - Bug fixes
- `docs/` - Documentation updates
- `refactor/` - Code refactoring
- `test/` - Test improvements

Example: `feat/thought-tree-provider`

## Performance Budgets

Maintain performance standards:

- Extension activation: < 200ms
- Command execution: < 100ms
- Tree view refresh: < 50ms
- Memory usage: < 50MB
- Bundle size: < 1MB

Profile regularly and optimize hot paths.

## Accessibility

Ensure extension is accessible:

- Proper ARIA labels in webviews
- Keyboard navigation support
- Screen reader compatible
- High contrast theme support
- Meaningful icon descriptions

## Extension Size

Keep extension lightweight:

- Bundle only necessary dependencies
- Tree-shake unused code
- Lazy load heavy features
- Compress assets
- Use VS Code's built-in libraries

Check bundle size:

```bash
npm run package
ls -lh *.vsix
```

## Deprecation Policy

When deprecating features:

1. Add `@deprecated` JSDoc tag
2. Log deprecation warning
3. Update documentation
4. Provide migration path
5. Remove after 2 major versions

## Security Best Practices

- Never commit secrets or tokens
- Validate all external input
- Use CSP in webviews
- Sanitize HTML content
- Review dependencies for vulnerabilities
- Use workspace trust API appropriately

Run security audit:

```bash
npm audit
```

## Continuous Improvement

Regularly review and update:

- Dependencies (monthly)
- VS Code API usage (check for deprecations)
- TypeScript version (yearly)
- Test coverage (maintain >80%)
- Documentation accuracy

## Quality Metrics

Track these metrics:

- Test coverage: >80%
- Build time: <30s
- Bundle size: <1MB
- GitHub issues response: <48h
- Bug fix time: <1 week
- Code review turnaround: <2 days
